[ ] 49 HTTP Request node -- react to a message and POST. Core, headless, medium.
       The existing Http Client (lib/pn-http.c:402) is a timer-driven GET with
       has_input=FALSE and no method selection, so you cannot make a request in
       response to an event, nor POST/PUT a body. The only outbound HTTP in the
       tree is the bespoke plugins/network/pn-https-tunnel-sender.c. Reuse the
       existing libsoup 3 plumbing (configure.ac:56; NO curl anywhere in the
       tree): pn-http.c:191 already builds its request as
       soup_message_new (SOUP_METHOD_GET, expanded) with the method as a plain
       argument, so the method becomes a property and a body attaches via
       soup_message_set_request_body_from_bytes. Template URL/body with
       ${path/to/field}; write data.output, data.status, data.success.

[ ] 51 Columns-to-Table node -- one generic text parser instead of N bespoke
       ones. Shell plugin, headless, medium. plugins/shell/pn-df-command.c,
       pn-lxc-ls-command.c and pn-free-command.c are three hand-written nodes
       whose only job is "run one fixed command, parse its columns into
       data.table"; TODO #12 asks for a fourth (pidstat|awk). Read data.output,
       write data.table, split by whitespace / delimiter / regex. Would let
       ShellCommand -> ColumnsToTable replace all of them and close #12 and #13
       without new C.

[ ] 52 Rolling Statistics node -- a sliding window over VALUES. Core, headless,
       medium. Throughput/Stats counts message ARRIVALS, not the values they
       carry, and Reduce folds one message's $pnvector without seeing a stream --
       so mean / min / max / stddev / rate-of-change exist nowhere. Every real
       sensor feed (rtl_433 temperature, Tasmota ENERGY.Power, System Load) is
       noisy and there is nothing to put between it and an AnalogMeter needle.

[ ] 53 Scale / Map-Range node -- retire the most-repeated idiom in the examples.
       Core, headless, small. examples/controls-and-logic/animation-test.json
       chains Expression("value * 10") -> Expression("value + 100"): two nodes
       for one affine map. Same pattern in examples/networking/crypto-price.json
       ("value + 12.0") and examples/controls-and-logic/knob.json. Four bounds
       in->out, plus clamp and round.

[ ] 54 Collect node -- turn a stream into a vector. Core, headless, medium. Only
       Ramp, Expression and Expression2 ever call pn_message_set_vector, so the
       ONLY way to obtain a vector is to synthesize one; nothing accumulates live
       readings for Reduce or Plot to consume. A hole in the analog-computer
       story: the scope shipped, but the only thing it can be fed is a signal we
       made up. Buffer data.value per message, flush on count / timeout / a
       second trigger input, emit one $pnvector.

[ ] 55 Function / Signal Generator node -- sine / square / triangle / sawtooth /
       sweep / noise. Core, headless, small. Subclass PnAutoTrigger for the
       sample tick; fields waveform, frequency, amplitude, offset, phase. The
       natural other half of the Oscilloscope (#44/#45) and a stimulus source for
       exercising a flow's Deadband/Stats/Threshold wiring with no live hardware.
       Sources today are Clock (time), AutoInjector (constant), AutoRandom
       (noise) and Ramp (vector) -- no deterministic time-varying waveform.

       OUTPUT SHAPE (decided): a MODE switch, scalar or vector. The consumers
       force this -- neither shape serves both halves of the node's purpose:

         [ ] 55.1 mode=sample (DEFAULT) -- one scalar per PnAutoTrigger tick on
               data.value. Feeds AnalogMeter / Dial / Numeric / Threshold /
               Deadband / the Sound chain, all of which read data.value as a
               double. This is the mode that serves the stimulus-source purpose
               above; a vector is meaningless to them.
         [ ] 55.2 mode=sweep -- one frame of N samples per tick as a SINGLE
               vector on data.value. Feeds Oscilloscope / Plot / Reduce, and is
               the synthetic counterpart of Collect (#54). Required, because a
               scalar stream cannot draw a waveform: the scope has exactly two
               states (pn-oscilloscope.c:124-127) -- snapshot (vy is the trace,
               vx an optional X vector) and point (vy==NULL: "the single latest
               (x,y) scalar, drawn as a dot. No history is kept"). Plot
               (pn-plot.c:294) and Reduce (pn-reduce.c:179) likewise resolve a
               vector or nothing.
         [ ] 55.3 ONE vector, not two. In sweep mode emit Y on data.value (the
               "universal slot", per pn-ramp.c:150-153 -- Ramp emits a vector
               there, AutoRandom a scalar, so both shapes on one key are already
               idiomatic). Make the X (time) vector OPTIONAL, under a
               configurable key the scope's x_key can point at: the scope already
               treats vx as optional and falls back to sample index, which is
               correct for a uniformly-sampled waveform, so an X vector buys only
               a real-seconds time axis. Cheap either way (pn_message_set_vector
               stores a $pnvector handle by reference, not a copy).
               NOT for X-Y: Lissajous needs two DIFFERENT signals, x=sin(at) and
               y=sin(bt+phi) -- two generators combined downstream, as
               examples/Compute/lissajous.json already does with Expression2. One
               node emitting x=time alongside y=sin(t) is a time-domain trace,
               not a Lissajous figure.
         [ ] 55.4 Known rough edge: in sample mode, wiring the generator straight
               into the Oscilloscope looks broken -- a dot crawling across the
               screen with an afterglow trail. That is the scope's missing roll /
               sweep mode (#45.3), NOT something this node should paper over by
               inventing its own history buffer.

