[x] 62 Optionally persist a graph's data with the worksheet. A Graph node
       reopened the next morning starts from a blank plot: everything it had
       collected lived only in RAM. New per-node boolean `save-data` (default
       FALSE -- a graph is a live view, and no document should start carrying
       a data set nobody asked for) writes the samples into the worksheet on
       save and reads them back on load. Bounded by construction, never
       "grow until the file is unopenable".
       [x] 62.1 The wire is a second, hidden read/write string property
             `saved-data` on the node itself, not a new PnNodeClass vfunc: the
             document serialiser already walks every writable subclass
             property, so this needs no ABI bump and no change to pn-flow.c.
             Its getter serialises on demand (and returns "" while `save-data`
             is off, which is what keeps the store out of every other
             worksheet); its setter parses and repopulates the rings. Absent
             from the PnSettingsSchema row list, so it is never a dialog row --
             only the `save-data` check button on the Data tab is.
       [x] 62.2 PnGraph. The rings are stamped with the MONOTONIC clock, which
             restarts with the process, so the format stores each sample as its
             age in ms plus the wall clock of the save; on load the two are
             added back into an age relative to now. A sample that aged past
             the resolution window while the file sat on disk is dropped, so a
             1-hour graph opened a day later comes back empty -- exactly what a
             live one would show. Two caps: in-window samples only, and at most
             PN_GRAPH_PERSIST_SAMPLES (512, >2 per bucket at the 200-bucket
             maximum) per series, times at most 12 series.
       [x] 62.3 Bin rebuild is deferred. The bucket width comes from
             `resolution` / `x-buckets` and the loader applies a node's
             properties in an unspecified order, so the restore folds the
             samples into the buckets straight away (a headless load never
             spins the main loop) AND once more from a one-shot idle, by which
             point every property in the file is in place. receive() and the
             restore share one series_bin_sample() so a reloaded sample lands
             exactly where a live one would.
       [x] 62.4 PnXYGraph gets the same pair, simpler: no time axis means
             nothing ages out, so the store is just the coordinates. Capped at
             `max-points` (all the plot draws anyway) and at
             PN_XY_GRAPH_PERSIST_SAMPLES (512) per series.
       [x] 62.5 Tests: round trip, the cap (and that it keeps the NEWEST
             samples), the default-off case, and malformed/empty input in
             tests/unit/test-pn-graph.c + test-pn-xy-graph.c, plus a real
             document save/load through pn_flow_save_to_file in
             test-pn-flow.c (which is what pins the feature end to end).
             Docs: data/help/PnGraph.html, data/help/PnXYGraph.html and the
             pipnode-nodes skill reference.
       [x] 62.6 Interaction with undo/redo, worth knowing: PnHistory snapshots
             the whole document through pn_flow_to_string, so with `save-data`
             on the samples ride inside every snapshot -- an undo therefore
             restores the plot as it stood at that step, the same way it
             already restores a Bridge Quote's cached quote. Snapshots are
             taken on document EDITS (the on_store_changed funnel), never per
             message, so a busy feed does not push history or mark the
             document dirty on its own.
       [ ] 62.7 Deferred: the other display nodes. Plot and Oscilloscope hold
             a SNAPSHOT (the last vector / the last trace), not accumulated
             history, so a reload repaints them from the next message anyway;
             they would need the same property pair only if a user wants the
             picture to survive a restart with no feed running.

[x] 63 Graphs (and every other plot) on the desktop layout. A Graph node was
       invisible to both halves of TODO #61: the Desktop Layout editor would
       not place it, and the viewer had no way to show one. The editor and
       the engine live in pipnode-editor and can call a node's own painter;
       the viewer deliberately links no pipnode runtime AND no plotting
       stack, which is what keeps a node crash out of its address space. So
       the editor draws the painter directly and the engine renders the SAME
       painter to a PNG for the viewer to blit -- one picture, one code path,
       no second implementation to drift.
       [x] 63.1 Eligibility is a vfunc test, not a type list:
             engine_is_plot_node() / layout_editor_is_plot() are
             "PN_NODE_GET_CLASS(node)->paint_plot != NULL, and no dedicated
             widget claimed it first". That covers Graph, XY Graph, Plot,
             Oscilloscope, Analog Clock, Analog Meter, Sun Path, Weather
             Report, Table View, Text View, Filedrop and any plugin node that
             paints, and a new painter needs no registration. The
             dedicated-widget test HAS to come first: Countdown and Label
             install paint_plot too, and their vector faces are the better
             mirror.
       [x] 63.2 Desktop only. A panel band is one icon tall and the panel
             layout has nowhere to store a size, so the panel builder still
             uses the narrow engine_is_widget_node(); only the desktop one
             widened to engine_is_mirrored_node().
       [x] 63.3 Per-widget size. PnLayoutPos gained w/h (0 = unset) with
             layout_get/set_size primitives and a public
             pn_flow_get/set_desktop_size pair; on disk a desktop_layout
             entry carries "w"/"h" ONLY when set, so a layout of nothing but
             readouts saves byte-identically to before (pinned by a test that
             counts the occurrences).
       [x] 63.4 Resize interaction. No second handle widget: the row's
             existing GtkEventBox reports a press inside the plot's
             bottom-right PN_DE_PLOT_GRIP square as a resize instead of a
             drag, and the grip is painted over the plot (dark stroke under a
             light one, so it reads on any plot). The pointer picks up
             "se-resize" over it and "move" everywhere else, through one
             row_set_cursor() that also replaced the old realize-time cursor
             code. Clamped to PN_DE_PLOT_MIN_* and to the window frame; a
             resize that ends where it began leaves the document clean, the
             same rule an unmoved drag follows.
       [x] 63.5 Transport: a "plot" widget state carrying w, h and a base64
             PNG the engine rendered at exactly w x h (cairo image surface ->
             write_to_png_stream -> g_base64_encode). The size travels with
             the picture rather than with the layout entry, so the widget
             reserves the right area before the first render arrives and a
             resized plot is never shown with its old picture stretched.
       [x] 63.6 Rendering is coalesced and gated, because it is the one
             widget state that costs real work. A plot's repaint-needed
             schedules ONE push PN_ENGINE_PLOT_PUSH_MS (200 ms) out and
             swallows every repaint until it fires; the pending source id
             lives on the node itself with a destroy notify, so a node
             finalized mid-wait cancels its own push instead of leaving a
             timeout holding a freed pointer (and engine_unwire_panel_widgets
             clears them all up front). Nothing is rendered for a plot the
             window would not show.
       [x] 63.7 Client half: panel-widgets/pn-plot-display.[ch]
             (PnPlotDisplay), the one widget in that library that restates
             nothing -- it decodes the PNG through GdkPixbuf, caches the
             surface, and re-decodes only when the encoded string actually
             changes. Empty/undecodable state paints a framed placeholder so
             a graph with no data yet reads as a reserved area. Mirror kind
             'p'; pn_widget_mirror_set_height is a no-op for it, so the
             viewer needed no change at all.
       [x] 63.8 Tests: two new cases in tests/unit/test-pn-flow.c (size round
             trip incl. resize-before-drag, and the disk round trip proving
             only the sized entry writes w/h), and the Graph added to
             tests/test_desktop_engine_run.py -- which decodes the PNG's IHDR
             rather than trusting the state's own w/h, so it proves the
             engine really rendered the painter at the layout's size. The
             panel engine test passes unchanged.
       [x] 63.9 Docs: a "Graphs and other plots" section in
             data/help/help-index.html (plus its TOC entry and the widened
             desktop_layout format note) and a paragraph in README.md.
       [ ] 63.10 Deferred: interaction. A plot in the window is a picture --
             the Oscilloscope's knobs and PnSunPath's drag-to-rotate do
             nothing there. The seam exists (ActivateWidget already carries a
             UUID for Switch/Inject); it would need a coordinate-carrying
             variant and a per-node "handle this click" vfunc.

[ ] 67 Topic Demux node -- the topic half of the #56 Router, built on the
       #66 multi-output ports. One input, N outputs (2..16, "outputs"), one
       topic pattern per output ("topics", a JSON array of strings). A message
       leaves unchanged by the FIRST output whose pattern matches; unmatched
       messages are dropped; an empty pattern matches nothing. Patterns with
       '*' or '?' are globs, otherwise MQTT filters ('+' one level, trailing
       '#' the rest). Core, Filters/Gate, fa-sitemap, Filter's yellow.
       [x] 67.1 lib/pn-topic-demux.[ch]: matcher (pure seam
             pn_topic_demux_topic_matches + pn_topic_demux_route), 16 fixed
             topic slots so shrinking the count keeps the patterns, output
             names follow the patterns (tail-ellipsized past 16 chars).
       [x] 67.2 Gui tier pn-topic-demux-gui.c: build_class_tab with the
             outputs spin + one entry per output, rebuilt on notify::outputs,
             refreshed on notify::topics (undo / D-Bus).
       [x] 67.3 Factory registration, help page, pipnode-nodes skill.
       [x] 67.4 Unit tests (tests/unit/test-pn-topic-demux.c).
       [x] 67.7 Functional test tests/test_topic_demux_route.py: the
             topic-demux example network built inline, run headless through
             pipnode-run, per-output routing + unlisted topic dropped.
       [ ] 67.5 GUI check by the user: the settings tab (spin grows/shrinks
             the topic fields live, typing relabels the output on the
             worksheet), long topic labels do not collide with the output
             value readout.
       [ ] 67.6 Candidate follow-up: migrate examples/home-automation/
             power.json and tasmota-socket.json from filter fans to a Topic
             Demux.
       [ ] 67.8 Candidate follow-up (from #56): migrate examples/networking/
             network_test.json's OnlySuccess / OnlyFail Filter pair to one
             Value Router (path "success", rules == 1 / == 0).

[ ] 68 Named-pipe nodes -- Pipe Reader (Sources) and Pipe Writer (Sinks) read
       and write a FIFO, one message per line. "pipe-path" names the FIFO
       (created when missing, a non-FIFO is refused and left alone);
       "format" is Output text (data.output, the default) or JSON message
       (the whole envelope, pn_message_serialize / _deserialize). Neither
       side ever blocks when the other end is absent or slow. Core, no gui
       tier (settings schema only), fa-sign-in / fa-sign-out, teal.
       [x] 68.1 lib/pn-pipe-common.[ch]: PnPipeFormat enum,
             pn_pipe_ensure_fifo (mkfifo / validate), pn_pipe_write (write
             with SIGPIPE blocked on the thread and a pending one consumed,
             so a vanished reader is EPIPE, not process death).
       [x] 68.2 lib/pn-pipe-reader.[ch]: O_RDONLY|O_NONBLOCK + our own
             O_WRONLY keep-alive end (writers may come and go without EOF /
             POLLHUP spinning the watch), g_unix_fd_add, line framing with
             a 1 MiB unterminated-line cap, open from an idle so failure
             messages reach wires; failures emit success=FALSE + has_error.
       [x] 68.3 lib/pn-pipe-writer.[ch]: lazy O_WRONLY|O_NONBLOCK open per
             message (ENXIO = no reader -> drop, not an error), bounded
             1 MiB whole-line queue drained by a G_IO_OUT watch, POLLERR
             probe so a reader that left and came back gets the next
             message, FIFO created up front from an idle on path set.
       [x] 68.4 Factory registration, help pages PnPipeReader.html /
             PnPipeWriter.html, pipnode-nodes skill (sources.md, sinks.md).
       [x] 68.5 Unit tests tests/unit/test-pn-pipe-reader.c and
             test-pn-pipe-writer.c (incl. slow-reader queue/drain and a
             writer -> FIFO -> reader JSON round trip).
       [x] 68.6 GUI check: both nodes in the palette (Sources / Sinks), the
             settings tab shows the file chooser + format combo, red until a
             path is set, `echo hi > fifo` lights a Pipe Reader, `cat fifo`
             shows a Pipe Writer's lines. Covered by
             tests/test_pipe_nodes_editor.py (68.9); only the red painting
             and the browse button's file chooser are left to the eye.
       [ ] 68.7 Candidate follow-up: surface the writer's dropped-message
             count on the node (it is only an API accessor today).
       [x] 68.8 "~" in file-path settings: lib/pn-path.[ch]
             pn_path_expand() ("~" and "~/rest" -> home, all else as is),
             applied at use time (the typed form is what is saved) in Pipe
             Reader / Pipe Writer, Logger, Text to Speech voice path, SSH
             profile identity file and the HTTPS Tunnel Receiver cert/key
             paths. tests/unit/test-pn-path.c; PLUGINS section 12 gotcha;
             help pages.
       [x] 68.9 Functional tests: tests/test_pipe_writer_reader.py (headless
             pipnode-run, AutoInjector -> Pipe Writer -> FIFO -> Pipe Reader
             -> Debug in both formats) and tests/test_pipe_nodes_editor.py
             (editor over D-Bus: palette, error state, dialog editors, the
             test script as the outside echo / cat). GetDialogEditorText /
             SetDialogEditorText now also drive PnFileChooserEntry and
             GtkComboBoxText editors.

[ ] 74 Keypad node (PnKeypad) -- a pocket-calculator key pad in the
       node's client area. Ten digits, ".", the four basic operators, "=", "C"
       and "CE"; clicking a key emits one message naming the key. The node does
       no arithmetic and holds no accumulator: it is an input device, and the
       natural partner downstream is the Calculator (PnExpression) node that
       evaluates what the pad types.

       [x] 74.1 Core lib/pn-keypad.[ch] (GTK-free): the static 4x5 layout
             table published in the header, pn_keypad_key_rect_in() as the one
             shared placement call, pn_keypad_hit_key(), pn_keypad_press() /
             pn_keypad_press_code(), and the transient pressed-key highlight.
             Sources category, fa-keyboard-o (NOT the Calculator's
             fa-calculator), no input port.
       [x] 74.2 Message shape: data.key (ASCII code -- the operator keys paint
             the typographic signs but emit "*" "/" "-" so the code drops
             straight into an expression string), data.kind (digit / point /
             operator / equals / clear / clear-entry) and data.value on the
             digit keys ONLY. No startup announce -- a keypad has no state to
             report.
       [x] 74.3 GUI tier lib/pn-keypad-gui.[ch]: the cairo case + keys +
             legends, placing every key through the core's shared rect call so
             the pixel seen and the key pressed cannot drift apart.
             paint_plot_skip_zoom so a body press hits a key instead of lifting
             the node into the zoom overlay.
       [x] 74.4 Worksheet routing: hit_test_keypad_key() + the press block
             beside the Switch / Inject button handling in pn-worksheet.c.
       [x] 74.5 Factory registration, gui install, help page PnKeypad.html,
             pipnode-nodes skill entry (sources.md).
       [x] 74.6 Unit tests tests/unit/test-pn-keypad.c -- layout completeness,
             label-vs-code, non-overlapping keys, the spanning "0" and "=",
             hit-test on every key and the deliberate misses, and the message
             each key family emits.
       [ ] 74.7 GUI check by hand: the pad paints in the palette and on the
             canvas, every key clicks and flashes, the header still selects /
             drags / double-click-opens settings, and the four colour settings
             repaint.
       [x] 74.8 An example worksheet wiring the pad into a calculator: done by
             #75, which added the Calculator Engine as that missing step and
             ships examples/controls-and-logic/calculator.json.

[ ] 75 Calculator Engine node (PnCalcEngine) -- the arithmetic half of a
       four-function calculator, and the "accumulate the keystrokes" step #74.8
       said had no single node. Reads one keystroke per message from data.key
       (what the #74 Keypad emits) and keeps accumulator + pending
       operator + typed entry. Keypad -> Engine -> Numeric is a working pocket
       calculator.

       [x] 75.1 Core lib/pn-calc-engine.[ch] (GTK-free, no gui tier -- the node
