       [x] 76.8.1 PnKeypadKey grew two fields: "sublabel" (a small second line
             under the legend, NULL on most keys) and "accent" (a PAINT hint,
             deliberately not a kind -- it is what lets the hex pad highlight
             A-F while still calling them digits). The painter picks the face
             colour off the flag instead of switch-ing on the kind.
       [x] 76.8.2 "Hex Keyboard": 4x4, 0..F counting left to right, A-F are
             kind "digit" with data.value 10..15 (g_ascii_xdigit_value, so the
             decimal pads are unaffected) -- a downstream accumulator folds a
             key in with acc * 16 + value. "C" is twelve here, NOT the
             calculator pad's clear key; the kind tells them apart.
       [x] 76.8.3 "Phone Keyboard": the decimal grid key for key, with the
             telephone letter groups under the digits (2 = ABC .. 9 = WXYZ).
             Lettered keys add data.letters; 1, 0, * and # stay bare. 188 px
             wide (vs the decimal pad's 152) so "PQRS" fits under the 7.
       [x] 76.8.4 The painter draws a sublabel as a smaller second line,
             centred with the digit as one block and blended 40% toward the
