[ ] 82 ANIMATION IN THE FIGURE NODE -- when an argument resolves to a
       VECTOR the figure becomes a film. This was 80.26, lifted out so
       that TODO #80 can close on the still figure it already draws
       correctly end to end; nothing about it is new, and nothing about
       it was started. The DESIGN stays where it was decided and is not
       copied here: 80.16 is the vector-is-a-film rule and its
       evaluate-once consequence, 80.17 is the controls. Read those two
       sub-points first, then this entry for what is left to build.

       The precondition 80.16 set is already met -- 80.22 through 80.25
       are done and a still figure draws -- so this is buildable now,
       not blocked.

       [ ] 82.1 THE FRAME COUNT, and it goes first because everything
             else indexes with it. 80.16 chose "the shortest vector"
             on the reasoning that vector OP vector truncates to the
             shorter operand; 80.3(f) was CORRECTED on 2026-09-22 after
             reading pn-var-store.c rather than its prose, and the real
             rule is the LONGER length with the surviving tail passing
             through verbatim -- [2,3] * [3,4,5] = [6,12,5]. So the
             figure cannot match its own arithmetic exactly whichever
             way it goes: under the tail rule a 2-vector times a
             3-vector is three frames whose last frame used no first
             operand at all. Shortest may well still be the right
             answer -- it just needs a new reason. Whatever is chosen,
             fix 80.16's parenthesis to match instead of leaving the
             two entries contradicting each other.
       [ ] 82.2 THE FRAME INDEX AT PAINT TIME (80.16a): evaluate the
             program ONCE for the whole film and take element i of
             every vector when painting frame i, scalars broadcasting
             unchanged. Not a re-run per frame -- PnVarStore is already
             elementwise, so the per-frame re-evaluation is both slower
             and further from what the language means.
       [ ] 82.3 THE TIMER, in the CORE half so the behaviour belongs to
             the node and not to the painter (80.17b): a g_timeout at
             1000/fps advances the index and calls
             pn_node_request_repaint(), stopped in dispose(), never
             started when the frame count is 1, and the index reset to
             0 when new data arrives. Plus 80.17(c)'s no-listener rule
             -- nothing connected to "repaint-needed" means headless,
             and a headless figure must not spin a timer nobody
             watches. Confirm the signal-handler check behaves as
             assumed at implementation time.
       [ ] 82.4 THE PROPERTIES (80.17a): `fps` (default 25, capped at
             60), `play-mode` (once / loop / ping-pong) and `frames`,
             an explicit count where 0 means "take it from the data".
             Plain GObject properties like the rest, so the save format
             picks them up, and rows in the node's existing settings
             schema.
       [ ] 82.5 `frame` AND `t` AS VECTORS (80.17d, 80.17e): bind
             `frame` as [0..N-1] and `t` as [0..1] so they animate
             through the same indexing as everything else, with no
             re-evaluation and no special case in the painter. The
             chicken-and-egg -- N comes from the data but `t` is part
             of it -- is answered by 80.2 rule 12's free-identifier
             walker: when `frames` > 0 the count is explicit and a
             figure with NO inputs can still animate, and when it is 0
             a second evaluation pass learns N first, run only for the
             programs that actually mention `t` or `frame`. At most two
             evaluations, only on input change.
       [ ] 82.6 TESTS AND HELP, in the shape 80.12 set: headless and
             pixel-free, asserting the frame count, the index-to-value
             mapping and the timer's no-listener rule in
             tests/unit/test-pn-figure.c rather than a new binary, and
             ./run-unit-tests.sh green. The help page 80.14 is still to
             write; whoever writes it covers animation from here, and
             if it is already written by then it gains a section.


[ ] 83 THE SECOND BATCH OF FUNCTIONS for the calculator language --
       the one PnVarStore shares with the Calculator, Calculator 2 and
       the Figure node (#80), so every name added here lands in all
       three at once. TODO #81 built the machinery and proved it on
       `atan2`; 81.11 is the rest of the list #81 itself settled --
       min, max, pow, hypot, asin, acos, atan, round, trunc, sign --
       and this entry is what comes after that: FORTY-FOUR NEW NAMES
       AND FIVE NEW CALL FORMS on names the language already has --
       forty-nine additions in all -- taken from three sources and no
       others.

       THE THREE SOURCES, because "add more functions" is how a
       language ends up with libm bolted to its side:
       (a) mcp-abacus (~/projects/mcp-abacus), whose
           `help(section="functions")` lists about fifty and which has
           already had the argument about what a calculator should
           know. It is a TYPE-FAITHFUL calculator -- fixed point,
           rational, float -- and much of its table exists to say
           where a result rounded; this language has exactly one
           numeric type, so we take its NAMES and SEMANTICS and none
           of its exactness apparatus.
       (b) libm, but only where a name is standard, one row long and
           answers a question the language cannot already answer.
       (c) THE WORKSHEETS THEMSELVES -- the idioms our own TODO
           entries have been hand-rolling. 79.6 spends a paragraph on
           sin(x)/x and its removable singularity, which is `sinc`.
           79.10's mask idiom is `if` written out in multiplication.
           #82's whole subject is interpolating between two numbers,
           which is `lerp`. 81.10 found 0.017453293 twice, which is
           `radians`. These are the best-earned rows in the entry
           because something already wanted them.

       Where a name means something in C, C wins -- the implementation
       is then one row pointing at libm and cannot be got wrong.

       THE CAP IS THE FIRST THING TO GO. 81.2 put a call's first
       argument in PnExprNode.left and its second in .right and capped
       the language at two, deliberately, because a third had nowhere
       to live and growing the public struct is an ABI hazard. 83.1
       shows the cap is representational rather than structural: an
       ARGUMENT CHAIN costs an appended enum value and not one byte of
       layout. That unlocks `clamp`, `lerp`, `smoothstep`, `if` and
       the annuity family, which is most of what makes this batch
       worth the trouble -- a two-argument language cannot express a
       CHOICE, and a drawing language that cannot choose is a
       straitjacket.

       DEPENDED ON 81.11 -- same table, same tests, same help -- and
       81.11 is DONE (2026-09-22), so this entry is unblocked. Two
       things it left behind are worth having before starting: the
       function list is written down in SEVEN places, not the four
       81.9 counted (see 81.11's finding), and
       test_all_builtin_functions now WALKS the table, so a row added
       without an assertion fails rather than passing quietly -- at
       forty-nine additions that guard is the difference between a
       tested batch and a hopeful one.

       THE MACHINERY IS BUILT (2026-09-22): 83.1, 83.2 and 83.19 are
       done, and two specimen rows came through them rather than none
       -- `clamp(x, lo, hi)` for the chain and `log(x[, base])` for the
       range -- the same way #81 shipped `atan2` with the comma. That
       is the entry's expensive half; everything still open below is a
       row, a decision, or a paragraph of help. NOTE for whoever
       continues: `clamp` is 83.11(b) and `log(x, base)` is one of
       83.7's six, both landed early and marked there.

       THE POLICY AND THE ANGLE FAMILY FOLLOWED (2026-09-22): 83.18
       settled first, on paper and in one paragraph, because every row
       with a domain obeys it -- then 83.3, 83.4, 83.5 and 83.6, which
       is eleven names and not one line of machinery. THIRTY-THREE
       functions in the table now. The help pages' flat list broke at
       that size, which 83.24 predicted, so the GROUPING it asks for
       landed with them: trigonometry, angles, hyperbolic, powers and
       logarithms, rounding and sign, choosing and bounding. Whoever
       adds the next row adds it to a heading, not to a paragraph.

       [x] 83.1 DONE (2026-09-22): THE ARGUMENT CHAIN. One value
             appended to PnExprNodeType -- PN_EXPR_NODE_ARG, .left =
             this argument, .right = the next -- so a call of any arity
             is a chain, exactly the way PN_EXPR_NODE_SEQ already
             chains statements. The precedent was in the same enum and
             it carried the whole change.
             (a) THE ABI STORY HELD, and it was checked rather than
                 argued: `git diff lib/pn-expr-parser.h` changes not
                 one field line -- sizeof (PnExprNode) is what it was,
                 no field changed meaning, and the only enum change is
                 an APPENDED value, the same class of change 81.3 made
                 to PnExprParserError. PN_PLUGIN_ABI_VERSION untouched,
                 no plugin rebuilt or reinstalled, no stale-dlopen
                 deadlock to warn anyone about.
             (b) UNIFORM, as decided: a two-argument call chains too,
                 so `atan2(1, 2)` is now CALL(.left=1, .right=ARG(2)).
                 The evaluator therefore REJECTS the old shape -- a
                 raw expression hanging off .right is "malformed
                 argument list", not a silently accepted call -- which
                 is what catches a stale hand-built tree, and is
                 pinned by its own case in test-pn-var-store.c.
             (c) THE WALKERS: confirmed, not assumed. pn-var-store.c's
                 CALL case was taught to walk the chain (and to collect
                 the argument NODES before evaluating any of them, so
                 the count is known first). lib/pn-figure.c needed
                 NOTHING, exactly as predicted -- is_foldable() and
                 collect_names() recurse into .left/.right generically.
                 Re-confirmed with a new test rather than by reading:
                 expr_three_args parses `circle 0, 0, clamp(r, 1, 10)`
                 and asserts `r` is still found as a free name, plus a
                 constant three-argument call and a ranged one folding
                 through the chain. pn_expr_node_free() already
                 recursed into both fields, so a chain frees itself.
             (d) PN_EXPR_MAX_ARITY is now a POLICY and its comment says
                 so: raised to 4, with the reason spelled out -- every
                 call in this language has a declared arity so the
                 parser can check it as the expression is typed (81.3),
                 and a bound keeps that cheap. The language could do
                 more and elected not to (83.21).

       [x] 83.2 DONE (2026-09-22): THE ARITY RANGE. PnExprFunc holds
             `min_arity` and `max_arity` instead of one `arity`, the
             parser checks the range and the evaluator dispatches on
             the count it actually got. The message is honest and comes
             from the table at BOTH ends -- one
             pn_expr_func_arity_phrase() builds "1 or 2 arguments",
             "3 arguments", "2 to 4 arguments", and the parser's
             complaint about a typed program and the evaluator's about
             a hand-built tree cannot drift apart. Position reporting
             is unchanged: the FUNCTION NAME's, per 81.3(a).
             (a) A ROW IS STILL ONE LINE, which a five-field struct
                 would have destroyed: PN_EXPR_FN1/FN2/FNN/FNR wrap the
                 shapes, so the table reads
                 `PN_EXPR_FN1 ("sin", sin)` and
                 `PN_EXPR_FNR ("log", 1, 2, expr_log)`. That matters
                 for a forty-nine-row entry more than it did for ten.
             (b) THE FIRST CUSTOMER came forward with it, because a
                 range with no ranged row is untestable machinery:
                 `log(x[, base])` from 83.7, which that entry already
                 said must be built WITH 83.2 rather than before it.
                 One argument is the natural log, two is
                 log(x)/log(base), one row, dispatching on the count.

       [x] 83.3 DONE (2026-09-22): HYPERBOLICS -- `sinh`, `cosh`,
             `tanh`. Three rows, three libm functions, no domain and no
             decision, exactly as written. 81.1 held them back with
             "until something asks for them" and the catenary is what
             asked: a hanging cable is cosh and nothing else, and it is
             exactly the kind of plate the Figure node exists to draw.
             Asserted against their definitions -- (e^x - e^-x)/2 and
             so on -- rather than at 0, where all three agree and a
             mis-wired row would pass.
       [x] 83.4 DONE (2026-09-22): INVERSE HYPERBOLICS -- `asinh`,
             `acosh`, `atanh`. Also libm, and the first rows to need
             83.18: acosh below 1 and atanh outside (-1, 1) are NaN,
             atanh AT 1 is +inf, and all three evaluate SUCCESSFULLY --
             the policy applied here first, as this entry predicted.
             Each is checked against its own forward function
             (asinh(sinh(0.7)) == 0.7), which is the assertion that
             catches a row pointing at the wrong libm name.
       [x] 83.5 DONE (2026-09-22): RECIPROCAL TRIG -- `cot`, `sec`,
             `csc`, written out the way `sign` is because none is in
             libm under that name: cos/sin, 1/cos, 1/sin. The POLES are
             documented and not special-cased, per this entry and
             83.18: cot and csc are +inf at 0 and infinite at every
             multiple of pi, sec a quarter turn away.
             ONE THING WORTH KNOWING, found by writing the test rather
             than by thinking about it: sec's pole is NOT REACHABLE
             from a program, because pi/2 is not exactly representable
             as a double -- sec(pi/2) is about 1.6e16, enormous but
             finite. The test asserts that rather than an infinity,
             which is the honest thing to pin and a reminder that a
             pole in a floating-point language is a cliff, not a wall.
       [x] 83.6 DONE (2026-09-22): `degrees` and `radians` -- the two
             most USEFUL names in the entry and the least mathematical.
             Every trig function takes radians while every knob, dial
             and compass bearing a worksheet carries is in degrees,
             which is why 81.10 found TWO examples with a hand-written
             0.017453293 in them. THE THIRD SWEEP of examples/ is done
             and it was one line: examples/Compute/projectile.json's
             `rad = angle * pi / 180` -- already improved once by
             81.10(b) -- now reads `rad = radians(angle)`, which is
             what it meant both times. lissajous.json's other constant
             is pi/2, a quarter turn and not a conversion, so it stays
             as it is; nothing else in examples/ converts an angle.
       [ ] 83.7 THE OTHER LOGARITHMS AND EXPONENTIALS -- `ln`, `log2`,
             `log(x, base)`, `exp2`, `expm1`, `log1p`. Six rows and
             one trap: the language's `log` IS the natural log
             (matching C and matching abacus), which surprises
             everyone who expects base ten, and `log10` is the one
             they wanted. `ln` is the unambiguous spelling of the same
             function and exists so a reader can be SURE; abacus
             carries it for that reason alone. `log(x, base)` is
             log(x)/log(base) and is the first name in the language
             with two arities, so it is built with 83.2, not before --
             and it WAS, landing with 83.2 on 2026-09-22 as that
             entry's proving customer, which leaves five rows here
             (`ln`, `log2`, `exp2`, `expm1`, `log1p`).
             `expm1`/`log1p` are the small-x pair -- exp(x)-1 and
             log(1+x) computed without the cancellation that makes the
             naive spelling lose every significant digit near zero --
             and they belong here because a worksheet computing a
             small interest rate or a decay over a short interval
             hits exactly that.
       [ ] 83.8 `cbrt` -- cube root, worth its own row because it is
             the one root that ACCEPTS NEGATIVES: cbrt(-8) is -2 while
             sqrt(-8) is NaN, an odd root being defined where an even
             one is not. pow(x, 1.0/3.0) gets the negative case wrong,
             which is the mistake this row exists to remove.
       [ ] 83.9 THE ROUNDING FAMILY, six additions and only two new
             names:
             (a) `round(x, n)`, `floor(x, n)`, `ceil(x, n)`,
                 `trunc(x, n)` -- the same four verbs the language
                 already has, given an optional SECOND argument that
                 rounds to n decimal places. No new rows; this is
                 83.2's arity range and its first customer.
             (b) `rint(x)` -- round to nearest with ties to EVEN,
                 which is what abacus's `round` does and what C's
                 round() deliberately does not (81.11 chose
                 half-away-from-zero). Both behaviours are wanted, so
                 both get a name and the help says which is which;
                 that is cheaper than arguing about one name.
             (c) `frac(x)` -- the fractional part, x - floor(x),
                 always in [0, 1). One line, and the sawtooth that
                 every phase accumulator and repeating animation in
                 #82 will want.
             Say plainly in the help that these are for QUANTISING a
             value before it travels on -- a price to the cent, a
             bearing to a tenth of a degree -- and not for formatting
             a number for display, which is the Numeric node's
             decimals property.
       [ ] 83.10 `fmod` and `copysign`. `fmod(x, y)` is C's TRUNCATED
             remainder and the language's `%` operator is FLOORED
             (the header says so: the result takes the divisor's
             sign), so they disagree on negative operands and both
             behaviours have their uses -- having only the operator
             means the other one gets hand-rolled wrongly.
             `copysign(x, y)` gives x the sign of y, the clean way to
             say "same direction as" in a figure without a comparison.
       [ ] 83.11 SELECTION AND SHAPING -- the five rows that need
             83.1's chain and the reason it is worth building:
             `if(cond, a, b)`, `clamp(x, lo, hi)`, `lerp(a, b, t)`,
             `step(edge, x)`, `smoothstep(lo, hi, x)`.
             (a) `if` is the one that changes the language. Today a
                 choice is written as 79.10's mask idiom, a
                 multiplication by a 1.0/0.0 comparison, and every
                 worksheet that needs one re-derives it. IMPLEMENT IT
                 AS A TRUE SELECT -- evaluate both arms, return one --
                 and NOT as cond*a + (1-cond)*b, or a NaN in the arm
                 nobody chose poisons the result. That distinction is
                 the entire value of the row; test it directly with
                 if(0, sqrt(-1), 1) == 1.
             (b) DONE (2026-09-22), early, as 83.1's specimen: the
                 chain needed a three-argument row to be judged on, and
                 `clamp` was the one with no open questions of its own.
                 It is the guard rail every gauge, dial and drawing
                 coordinate wants, and min(max(x, lo), hi) is what
                 people write instead and get the argument order wrong
                 in. DECIDED, as this entry demanded: crossed bounds
                 (hi < lo) return LO, whichever side x is on, and a NaN
                 VALUE passes through as NaN rather than becoming a
                 bound -- which the fmin/fmax spelling would have done
                 silently, the same mistake `sign` refuses in 81.11.
                 Both pinned in test-pn-var-store.c and written into
                 both help pages. The other four rows here -- `if`,
                 `lerp`, `step`, `smoothstep` -- are untouched, and
                 `if` still wants 83.20's finding in its help before it
                 is built.
             (c) `lerp(a, b, t)` = a + (b-a)*t, the straight line
                 between two numbers. #82 is ENTIRELY about animating
                 between values, and with `t` already bound as a
                 vector over [0, 1] (82.5) a whole animation becomes
                 lerp(start, end, t) on one line. Use the a + (b-a)*t
                 spelling rather than (1-t)*a + t*b so that lerp with
                 t = 1 returns b exactly.
             (d) `step(edge, x)` is 0 below the edge and 1 at or above
                 it -- the mask idiom given a name -- and
                 `smoothstep(lo, hi, x)` is its eased twin, the
                 classic 3t^2-2t^3 with the ends clamped. Together
                 with (c) they are the easing vocabulary #82's
                 animations will otherwise hand-roll.
       [ ] 83.12 CLASSIFICATION -- `isnan`, `isinf`, `isfinite`,
             returning the language's usual 1.0/0.0. Small rows with a
             specific job: this language lets a NaN travel (83.18),
             and without these there is NO WAY to test for one --
             x != x works in C and is exactly the trick nobody should
             have to know. With `if` from 83.11 they become a usable
             guard: if(isfinite(v), v, 0).
       [ ] 83.13 THE SPECIAL FUNCTIONS, five rows earned by the physics
             sheets rather than by completeness:
             (a) `sinc(x)` = sin(x)/x with the removable singularity
                 handled -- sinc(0) is 1, not NaN. TODO #79.6 spends a
                 whole paragraph on this exact 0/0 and offsets its
                 sample grid by 1e-9 to dodge it; this row is the
                 real fix, and the diffraction sheet's envelope IS
                 sinc. Use the unnormalised form (sin(x)/x, not
                 sin(pi*x)/(pi*x)) and say which in the help, because
                 the two conventions differ and both are common.
             (b) `erf`, `erfc` -- libm, one row each, and the shape of
                 every diffusion, noise and measurement-error curve
                 there is. The Gaussian's integral has no elementary
                 spelling, so without these it cannot be written at
                 all.
             (c) `j0`, `j1` -- Bessel functions of the first kind,
                 also libm. Niche, except that they are precisely what
                 #79's sheet needs the moment it moves from a slit to
                 a CIRCULAR aperture: the Airy pattern is j1, the way
                 the slit's is sinc. Two rows for a whole second
                 plate.
       [ ] 83.14 `factorial`, `gcd`, `lcm` -- the integer-minded three,
             in a language with one double type, so each has to say
             what it does with a fractional argument. Abacus refuses,
             which is more honest than truncating; follow it.
             factorial also needs a cap: 171! overflows a double to
             +inf, so about 170 is the natural limit here and anything
             above it is an error rather than a silent infinity.
             Negatives refuse. gcd and lcm are the TWO-argument forms
             only (abacus spells both variadic, see 83.21), sign
             dropped, and lcm of anything with 0 is 0.
       [ ] 83.15 THE PERCENT FAMILY -- `pct(x, p)` = x*p/100,
             `pct_change(old, new)` = (new-old)/old, `bps(x, b)` =
             x*b/10000. Trivial arithmetic, which is the point: they
             exist so nobody hand-rolls /100 and nobody confuses a
             percent with a basis point. Two warnings for the help:
             pct_change divides by zero when `old` is 0, and the
             argument order is old-then-new, which is the one thing
             about it that can silently be got backwards.
       [ ] 83.16 THE ANNUITY FAMILY -- `compound(principal, rate,
             periods)`, `fv(pmt, rate, nper)`, `pv(pmt, rate, nper)`,
             `pmt(pv, rate, nper)`. Four three-argument rows, straight
             from abacus and unreachable before 83.1. They are here
             because a worksheet that reads an energy meter is one
             expression away from reading a bill, and because each is
             a formula people get wrong from memory. Two things the
             help must state, as abacus's own text does: the rate is
             PER PERIOD and `periods` counts the same unit, and the
             r = 0 case is not a division but a limit (pmt*nper), so
             each row special-cases it rather than returning inf.
       [ ] 83.17 `time()` -- current Unix epoch seconds, a
             ZERO-argument function and the only impure name abacus
             carries. Listed to be DECIDED, not assumed: the language
             is otherwise a pure function of its inputs, an expression
             that changes value when nothing arrived breaks the
             evaluate-once rule #82 depends on, and pipnode already
             has a Clock node whose whole job is to put a timestamp on
             a wire. The recommendation is to LEAVE IT OUT and say so
             in the help beside the Clock node's name. If it does go
             in it is also the first zero-argument call, and `time()`
             with an empty argument list is something 81.3(c)
             explicitly made an error -- so it is a grammar change,
             not a row.
       [x] 83.18 DONE (2026-09-22): THE NaN AND DOMAIN POLICY, settled
             ONCE for the whole batch and BEFORE the rows that need it,
             which is what this entry asked for. The rule, in the one
             paragraph it asked for and now written in both help pages,
             the table's own comment and pn-var-store.h:
               A RESULT MATHEMATICS DOES NOT DEFINE IS A VALUE, NOT AN
               ERROR. sqrt(-1), acosh(0), atanh(2) and asin(2) are NaN;
               log(0), cot(0) and csc(0) are infinite; each evaluates
               SUCCESSFULLY and travels on down the wire, exactly as
               `1 / 0` already did before this entry. Nothing in the
               table raises.
             The reason is the Figure node's reason: a gap in one
             sample of a curve must not take the whole plate down with
             it -- and 79.6's removable singularities are the same
             argument from the other side.
             WHAT MAY STILL RAISE, when those rows land, is an argument
             wrong in KIND rather than out of range -- factorial(-1),
             factorial(1.5), gcd(1.5, 2) (83.14). The test to apply
             there: does the question have an answer this language can
             carry? "The square root of -1" has one, and NaN is how a
             double says "not a real number"; "the factorial of a half"
             has none, because that is a different function. Anything
             that does raise uses the existing error path so the node
             lights up the way a parse error does.
             Pinned by domain_policy in test-pn-var-store.c -- eight
             undefined results asserted to be NaN or infinite AND to
             evaluate without an error -- so the policy cannot drift
             into a raise by accident.
       [x] 83.19 DONE (2026-09-22): THE VECTOR RULE, generalised and
             still structural. zip_value() became zipn_value() over N
             operands and map_value()/map2_value() were DELETED as
             separate implementations -- a unary map is now a
             one-operand zip and arithmetic is a two-operand one, so
             `~a`, `sin(a)`, `a * b`, `atan2(a, b)` and
             `clamp(a, b, c)` all broadcast through the same function
             and a call CANNOT drift from an operator. One Kernel
             carries the operator code or whichever of the three
             function shapes the row declares.
             THE DECISION THE GENERALISATION FORCED, which two
             operands never had to make: with three, an index where one
             operand has run out can have SEVERAL survivors, so "the
             surviving tail passes through verbatim" has to say which.
             It is the FIRST argument that still has an element. At
             n == 2 that is exactly today's rule (only one can
             survive), and at three or more it is the reading that
             keeps the promise, because in every row of this entry
             argument one is the VALUE and the rest are parameters --
             so a bound vector shorter than the value it bounds leaves
             the rest of the value alone rather than clamping it
             against nothing. Pinned by vector_fn_3arg, and written
             into both help pages' vector sections.

       [ ] 83.20 A FINDING TO RECORD BEFORE `if` IS BUILT, because it
             limits what 83.11(a) can promise: a COMPARISON in this
             language collapses a vector to a single 1.0/0.0 -- true
             only when every element passes, as both help pages state
             -- so `if(v > 0, a, b)` has a SCALAR condition and
             chooses once for the whole vector, not per element.
             That is also why 79.10's mask idiom is a scalar gate
             rather than a mask. Per-element selection would need an
             elementwise comparison, which is a language change and a
             separate entry; for now write the limitation into `if`'s
             help paragraph rather than letting someone discover it
             with a wrong picture on screen.
       [ ] 83.21 WHAT IS STILL LEFT OUT, and note that 83.1 changes the
             REASON for the first one:
             (a) THE VARIADIC ONES -- avg, median, stddev, variance,
                 and the unbounded spellings of min, max, gcd, lcm.
                 The argument chain makes them REPRESENTABLE, so this
                 is now a choice rather than a limit: a fixed arity is
                 checkable at parse time, which is the property 81.3
                 was built for and the one that lights a node up as
                 the expression is typed. Leaving them out keeps every
                 call in the language checkable. Revisit deliberately
                 or not at all.
             (b) THE TEMPTING RE-READING of (a): in THIS language a
                 vector is a single value, so avg(v), median(v) and
                 stddev(v) could be UNARY functions reducing a vector
                 to a scalar. Real, attractive, and still not this
                 entry -- it breaks the rule 83.19 calls structural
                 (functions map, they do not reduce), and pipnode
                 already has a Reduce node that sums, averages and
                 takes the extrema of a vector on a wire. A separate
                 entry with its own argument, not a row.
             (c) THE SYMBOLIC ONES -- sum, product, diff, integral.
                 They take an UNEVALUATED expression and a bare
                 variable NAME as arguments, which is a different
                 language from this one; nothing in the parser can
                 express "do not evaluate this yet". 83.1's chain does
                 not help, because the problem is evaluation and not
                 arity.
             (d) NO NEW OPERATORS, restating 81.7 because a batch this
                 size is exactly when one gets smuggled in: `^` stays
                 XOR, there is no `**`, and squaring is still a
                 multiplication or pow(x, 2). This entry ADDS NAMES
                 and changes nothing about what an existing worksheet
                 means.
       [ ] 83.22 NAMES MUST MATCH ABACUS WHERE BOTH HAVE THEM, because
             the user drives both and a name meaning two things is
             worse than a name existing in only one. Three known
             divergences to record rather than discover later: abacus
             documents `atan2(x, y)` while the language shipped C's
             `atan2(y, x)` in 81.1 -- C's order stays and the help
             says which way round it is; abacus's `round` is
             ties-to-even while 81.11 chose half-away-from-zero, which
             83.9(b) resolves by giving the other behaviour its own
             name; and abacus's `log(x, y)` two-arg form must mean
             base y here too, not something cleverer. Walk the
             finished table against abacus's list once more before
             closing the entry.
       [ ] 83.23 TESTS, in the shape 81.8 set and in the SAME binaries
             rather than new ones. test-pn-var-store.c gets a value
             assertion per row, the vector broadcast for every
             multi-argument function, and the zipN tail rule at arity
             3 and 4. test-pn-expr-parser.c gets the arity cases,
             which for this entry are the RANGE errors from 83.2 (the
             first in the language that are not a single number) and
             the CHAIN from 83.1 -- a four-argument call, a nested
             call in the third position, and one argument too many.
             THE MACHINERY'S SHARE IS DONE (2026-09-22):
             arity_three_range and vector_fn_3arg in
             test-pn-var-store.c, the range and cap errors in
             test-pn-expr-parser.c's call_arity (with the message TEXT
             asserted, not only the code), expr_three_args in
             test-pn-figure.c for 83.1(c), and both specimens in the
             exhaustive table walk. What is left here is one assertion
             per ROW as the rows land.
             Each decision taken in 83.11(a), 83.14, 83.18 and 83.9(b)
             gets an assertion, because a policy nobody tested is a
             policy that drifts. Headless, pntest.h,
             ./run-unit-tests.sh green.
       [ ] 83.24 HELP, and 81.9 already counted the places the function
             list is written down -- PARTLY DONE (2026-09-22): the
             GROUPING this entry asks for is in both node help pages
             already, because the flat list stopped being readable at
             thirty-three names, and the NaN paragraph from 83.18 is
             written beneath it. What is left here is a heading for
             each group still to come and the other two paragraphs
             (83.20's scalar-condition limitation on `if`, and the
             `^`-is-XOR trap, which is already in the powers group).
             The places: data/help/PnExpression.html and
             PnExpression2.html (the list AND the vector section in
             each), the grammar paragraph on pn_expr_parser_parse in
             pn-expr-parser.h, and pn-var-store.h. PnFigure.html --
             which 81.9 could not update because it did not exist --
             is 80.14 and by then should. At forty-nine additions a
             flat
             comma-separated list stops being readable, so group them
             on the page the way this entry groups them, with a
             one-line gloss per name: the reader looking for `radians`
             is looking for a heading, not for a word in a paragraph.
             Three paragraphs are worth more than the list itself --
             the NaN policy from 83.18, `if`'s scalar-condition
             limitation from 83.20, and the `^`-is-XOR trap pointing
             at pow, which 81.11 already owes the page.
